      title: "\"Slut!\" turns out to be a love song",
      summary: "The vault title everyone braced for arrived as a dreamy synth-pop ballad about a romance worth the name-calling — she'd once had to choose between it and \"Blank Space\" for the original 1989.",
      body: ["Swift said the two songs both \"cheekily play on the discussions at that time of my life around my dating life\" — Blank Space made the 2014 cut, and \"Slut!\" waited nine years.", "It debuted at No. 3 on the Hot 100, and critics read the soft, tender arrangement as the point: reckoning with the slut-shaming era by refusing to write an angry song about it."],
      tags: ["Music"],
      images: [{ url: "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/d/d5/Taylor_Swift_-_1989_%28Taylor%27s_Version%29.png", credit: "Republic Records", caption: "1989 (Taylor's Version), where \"Slut!\" surfaced from the vault as a love song instead of the expected diss.", kind: "archival" }],
      sources: [{ name: "\"Slut!\"", url: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%22Slut!%22" }, { name: "The Sadness of 'Slut' — And More of Our Takeaways from Taylor Swift's New Vault Tracks", url: "https://www.rollingstone.com/music/music-features/taylor-swift-1989-vault-tracks-revelations-1234864433/" }],
